The IRC medium is a public medium, and most administers will argue
that there is no such thing as 'channel ownership' on the IRC network.
For the most part, I agree with this philosophy, and believe anything
that one says on IRC *is* protected under the same guidelines as, say,
standing in front of an auditorium or shouting out in a crowd.
However, even in the 'Real World' there are limitations to these
freedoms.  

>If this happened on usenet in an unmoderated list we would say tough
>luck free speech.  It couldn't happen on a moderated list (except to the
>moderator which is another issue).  But since a channel can be controlled
           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>somewhat and you could make the case that this intrudee was not an
>invited guest.

Actually, the question of a moderator being the *cause* of an IRC
channel's problem *is* a frequent issue when these questions come up.
To become a 'channel operator', either 1) you must be the one that
created the channel, or 2) another channel operator must have passed
this status to you.  If someone gets the channel op status and then
abuses it, it's generally considered that the fault is with the people
that gave her the opportunity.

All this aside, channels are pretty well protected against the
'average' bozo trying to disrupt things.  Anyone can use the 'ignore'
command if the intruder is being offensive.  Anyone with channel op
status can use the 'kick' command to boot the offender off the
channel.  And if she keeps comeing back, anyone with channel op status
can add her to the ban list, keeping her off the channel until someone
removes her.  What's more, post channels have the 't' and 'n' flags
set which keep people from disrupting a channel from outside, and from
setting the topic without channel op.  There are even more flags that
can be set for invite only, put a limit on the number of participants,
channel op moderation, and others.

But even with all these safeguards against casual abuse, IRC is still
not considered a private medium.  Any determined individual can tap
into the words being typed, or even break past the electronic barriors
that protect IRC channels.  It is possible to communicate directly
from client to client, but this is (as of yet) not implemented very
well and is still pretty easy to crack.

What all this blils down to is that if the Nth order of Zog wanted to
hold a meeting using the IRC medium and established servers, they'd
have about as much privacy as a Nudist Nuns convention in the
middle of Times Square.

