In article <1ptn0e$4rk@merlin.resmel.bhp.com.au> djk@bhppmel.bhp.com.au (David Keegel) writes:

> Can you tell us *why* Georgia Tech does not carry the alt groups?
> If it is their policy not to allow anyone there access to them,
> I think it would be wrong for us outsiders to suggest anything which
> would enable you to break the rules of your organization.

Okay, it is wrong, but your policy then is even more wrong. Of all
cases that I know, such restrictions (the more global they are, the
higher the probablilty) are mostly given out of no other reasons than
the will to 'get things under control', read to get people under
control. Technical reasons are mostly not given since they are not
there.

If they for example don't carry the alt groups because of volume/
storage concerns, they are wrong since not all alt groups are big and
there are big non-alt groups too. If they say 'alt is all about
illegal or questionable concerns', they are wrong (mail me for
references.) And so on.

I suggest following procedure:
first *talk* to the people responsible. Don't get around the
restrictions with what could be considered 'disrupting dirty tricks'.
Ask them about *why* the restrictions are there. If you think they are
invalid, tell them why. Tell other people with influence in your
organization too.

If all that fails and all ppl responsible show no understanding of
*valid* arguments, then you could try to get around.

> If I was a network administrator and found that some dept's users were
> using NNTP to a foreign country, I would be a *very* unhappy person.

Of technical concerns, I assume, since this sort of around-globe
access is unnecessary and wasteful. (But just think over the much
bigger problem of unnecessary FTP, especially in Europe...)
If it is just of political reasons, you'd better overthink your
policy.

Olaf
