[...]

The way to fight offensive speech and hate speech is to out compete
it, not to outlaw it. If you outlaw it, you only push it underground,
if you out compete it, you wither it with the light of reason.

If you outlaw it, you give the authorities the dangerous power to
decide what is and isn't "offensive". If you out compete it, you
exercise the power of a free person to search for truth without
government barriers. (If you live in a country were the authorties can
be trusted with the power to decide what is and isn't offensive, I
envy you.)

As the American Association of University Professors says: "On a
campus that is free and open, no idea can be banned or forbidden.  No
viewpoint or message may be deemed so hateful or disturbing that it
may not be expressed."

At state universities in the U.S., free expression is not just a good
idea; it is the law. The U. of Wisconsin and the U.  of Michigan have
tried to ban "hate speech". The federal courts said the bans were
illegal.

I'm enclosing pointers to on-line material on these points. I commend
those posters who attacked the hateful message rather than the freedom
that allowed it to be expressed.

