The University of Washington is a state university. In the U.S. that
means that it *is* the government, bound by the 14th Amendment not to
violate its students 1st Amendment rights.

Also, most universities, both public and private, in the U.S.  (and
Canada?) promise in their student code that students will be free of
institutional censorship. This is a legally-binding, contractual
obligation.

[...]
>	So I think it is inappropriate for the Director of the
>University Computing Services to cite "freedom of speech" as the
