I just saw the post about "Not moving to Canada" and couldn't resist throwing
in a bit more.  While this is not necessarily a "nice" thing to do I think
it represents the frustrations of people in all of the western world.  A few
years ago I had the oportunity to spend some time in Europe and get to know
a few of the "locals" in various countries, including Germany, Austria, France,
and Czechoslovokia.  Their views on the immigration situation are similar to
what the authors' of the "Canada" post are.

No one ever said life was fair.  Whether these people are escaping oppresion,
poverty or whatever, is beside the point.  I myself feel sorry for people in
these situation, and if I could change it I would, but I think that allowing
large migrations of people into more economically stable western countries,
we are in fact lowering our own standard of living.  This defies the sub-
conscious human trait of greed, and makes us feel threatened.  Even in the
depressed economy of Czechoslovokia, the people with whom I discussed similar
issues felt the same way.  They did not want to even give up what little they
had.  Recently on the news, I saw that France was addressing their immigration
"problem" by restricting the number of people allowed into their country, but
yet there is a public outcry in the United States when this is brought up.

I think ties can also be drawn to the immigration of Haitians and Cubans with
the AIDS virus being allowed to come to the U.S. for medical treatment.  Why
is it our responsibility to take care of the rest of the world?  Surely these
people have no money to pay for their treatment, but yet our government finds
in necessary to grant this "gift" when there are thousands upon thousands of
American citizens without means as well, but do not recieve help.

Just my two cents worth.....

mtn4583@silver.sdsmt.edu

