Campus Report articles from the archives of Accuracy in Academia (AIA)
Sent independently of AIA by a loyal supporter of the organization's goals and 
purpose.
--------------------------------------------------------
 CREDIT WHERE NONE IS DUE

 taken from Campus Report, Accuracy In Academia, May 1991

  A bumper sticker reads: "They can send me to college, but the
 can't make me think."  That appears to be true, considering:
  * A 1989 survey by the National Endowment for the Humanities
 found that a quarter of graduating college seniors could not
 distinguish a quotation of Winston Churchill from that of Josef
 Stalin or Karl Marx.  Half of those surveyed could not understand
 the purpose of the Emancipation Proclamation or The Federalist
 Papers.
  *  A 1988 National Geographic survey of high school students
 found that a majority could not locate Argentina, Chile, or Peru
 on a map.  On the homefront, they also could not locate Ohio,
 Michigan, or New Jersey.
  * A 1986 National Endowment for the Arts survey of high school
 juniors  found that two-thirds couldn_t place the date of the
 Civil War within a 50-year period.
  Citizenship is another area in which American students have been
 found to be lacking.  The People for the American Way report,
 _Democracy_s Next Generation,_ found: _Young people cherish
 America_s freedoms without understanding what it takes to preserve
 them_ and _Institutions with the best opportunity to teach young
 people citizenship_family, school, and government_have let them
 down._
  One would think that an alarmed American educational
 establishment would start setting a course to right these
 wrongs_pushing forth on a new agenda that would strengthen the
 content of coursework and provide more incentives for students to
 study.  There has been, however, little action on this front, but
 some that has is proving to be of more detriment than help.
  In a recently-released report from the National Endowment for the
 Humanities, _Tyrannical Machines,_ pointed out the problems of
 educational reforms.  The report stated: _Practices that begin by
 filling needs become detached from their original purposes, even
 counterproductive to them.  Having been adopted on a large scale,
 however, these practices take on a power of their own.  We force
 prospective schoolteachers to take education courses that waste
 their time.  We place expectations on college and university
 faculty members that discourage them from devoting time to
 students and the classroom.  Thoughtful teachers and faculty
 members, as well as concerned administrators, chafe under these
 regimes; but the machines, larger than any individual or school,
 roll on._
  Multiculturalism is becoming a sink or swim factor in the world
 of academe.  Even as political correctness is being unmasked,
 inclusive curricula, hiring quotas, and victim_s studies are
 becoming required by some academic accreditation services.
  The Association of American Law Schools has required that member
 schools deny assistance to employers discriminating on the basis
 of sexual orientation.  This prompted many law schools to kick the
 CIA, FBI, and military off their campus, depriving students of the
 opportunity to interview with the employer of their choice.  This
 AALS directive has been overruled at the University of Kansas and
 Drake University after Marine Captain Patrick L. Redmon discovered
 a law prohibiting colleges from hindering military recruitment
 while receiving Department of Defense funding (see February, 1991,
 Campus Report), and  the Virginia legislature helped overturn the
 AALS policy at the University of Virginia (see April, 1991, Campus
 Report).
  Education Secretary Lamar Alexander discovered political agendas
 creeping into the accreditation process.  The Middle States
 Association of Colleges and Schools chose to defer accreditation
 to Baruch College and Westminster Theological Seminary because
 they did not conform to the Middle States agenda, an agenda that
 implies that hiring should be done by a quota system taking race
 and sex into account, and that alleged victims of oppression make
 the best teachers because they help raise the self-esteem of
 fellow minorities and reinforce the notion that there is no
 unifying culture.
  Alexander noted that Middle States prerequisites _may undermine
 institutional autonomy and academic freedom and could in fact
 lessen variety among the nation_s colleges and universities_ and
 that such prerequisites _could conceivably cause violations of
 federal civil rights laws by, for example, leading to race-based
 hiring quotas._
  Students aren_t helping turn the tide.  A proposed ethnic studies
 requirement at the University of California at Davis was
 criticized by radical students as weak.  The member of the campus
 chapter of the National Organization for Women called the proposal
 for one course on _underrepresented and historically discriminated
 against groups_ and another on _the history of racism and
 discrimination in the United States_ a _piece of s_._
  When students are having problems finding where they are on a map
 and are unable to understand the founding principles of the United
 States, the educational system does not need to try to teach them
 a radical distortion of the _history of racism._  Wouldn_t it help
 America to be more competitive if every student were required to
 take a course in economics?  Or some form of computer science?
 Some schools already have curriculums that require these courses,
 but some accreditation agencies seem to be looking more into
 requiring diversity than the three R_s.
