In article <1993Apr7.165210.9928@midway.uchicago.edu> thf2@midway.uchicago.edu writes:
>
>The Supreme Court has been awfully unfriendly lately to the idea of
>extending the concept of the "public forum" beyond streets and parks.
>Still, even with semi-public fora, my understanding is that restrictions
>have to be time/place/manner content-neutral ones.
>
>The question then arises whether a network is semi-public or private,
>which turns on inquiries that I am less than completely familiar with,
>and thus reluctant to comment upon.
>--

  Ted is right at least in part. It seems very unlikely that networks would  
be found to be public fora by the current court.  The expansion of that 
definition was primarily the crusade of Brennan and Marshal and they pretty
much lost.  Public forum is now, for all intents and purposes, a synonym for
"street or park." <Did you have Kagan for 1st Amendment?>
  Anyway, all of this is irrelevant in light of the original poster's    
question which suggested that universities were censoring postings because
they contatined opinions which reflected badly on the university.  If that
is the case, the law is the same for public, non-public or semi-public (if
such a thing exists) fora:  such censoring is viewpoint based and will
likely be struck down.      
  Several caveats here (besides the standard disclaimers about not being a
lawyer yet):  First, we must assume the university is in whole or in part
state funded.  If it's a private actor, of course, it can do as it pleases.
Second viewpoint-based is not the same content-based--that is, if the net is  
not a public forum, the university can impose a blanket policy that says 
"we won't publish anything that discusses out affirmative action program" (a
content based restriction) but it cannot have a policy that says "we will
not publish any _unfavorable_ articles about our affirmative action program."
This is the distinction articulated in Greer v Spock (424 US 828 (1976)) and
other formative public forum cases.  [And all of this is in the process of  
breaking down, by the way, under the confused 1st Amend. jurisprudence of the
current CJ, see Renton v Playtime Theaters (475 US 41 (1986)).  The third      
caveat is that the entire INTERNET may belong to the government and I'm not 
sure how this would effect the analysis.  It's not clear who owns the internet,
not like the good old days where it was ARPAnet and the DOD owned it and
that was that.  Sometimes the government acts as if it still has some
ownership interest in the Internet (as in the Robert T. Morris case, the site
for which I don't have handy) and sometimes it doesn't.  Also, I haven't read
Cubby v Compuserve so I don't know how broad the common carrier logic is, but
if it applies to the internet that might also effect the ability of its
administartors (the universities) to censor matierial.  Gee, with all these
caveats there's not much left of my original $0.02.  Oh well.        

  Thanks for listenin'
  Steve
  
