Not to worry. We're long past the days when any government entity could
claim to "own" the Internet.

>Sometimes the government acts as if it still has some
>ownership interest in the Internet (as in the Robert T. Morris case, the site
>for which I don't have handy) and sometimes it doesn't.

The Morris case did not assume that the government had any ownership
interest in the Internet. 18 USC 1030 broadly defines "federal interest
computers." The issue was the Worm's effect on "federal interest
computers," not on the Internet per se.

> Also, I haven't read
>Cubby v Compuserve so I don't know how broad the common carrier logic is, but
>if it applies to the internet that might also effect the ability of its
>administartors (the universities) to censor matierial.  Gee, with all these
>caveats there's not much left of my original $0.02.  Oh well.        

Cubby doesn't depend on common-carrier analysis.


